The root cause of the bug is that the function `gpiochip_set_desc_names()` no longer enforces uniqueness for GPIO line names, leading to potential collisions. This change means that multiple GPIO lines can have the same name, which can cause confusion and incorrect behavior when retrieving GPIO descriptors using the `gpio_name_to_desc()` function, as it will return the first match found without indicating that there are duplicates.

Code statements information:
The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
// No longer checks for unique GPIO line names
static int gpiochip_set_desc_names(struct gpio_chip *gc)
```
```c
// Returns the first match found without indicating duplicates
static struct gpio_desc *gpio_name_to_desc(const char * const name)
```
Reason:
The function `gpiochip_set_desc_names()` has been modified to allow non-unique GPIO line names, which means that multiple GPIO lines can share the same name. Consequently, when `gpio_name_to_desc()` is called, it will return the first matching GPIO descriptor it finds, leading to potential confusion and incorrect behavior due to the lack of indication of duplicate names.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
+ * Note that there is no guarantee that GPIO names are globally unique!
```
